<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <!--
    SEL LIVE Windows Agent — the single setup program.

    ── What this is, and why it exists alongside the MSI ─────────────────────────────────────────

    Package.wxs produces an MSI that installs the agent. It cannot install the agent's
    prerequisites, because an MSI cannot chain another MSI: Windows Installer serialises
    installations, so a package that tried to launch .NET Framework 4.8 from a custom action
    would deadlock against itself. That is not a WiX limitation, it is the engine's.

    So the MSI checks for .NET 4.8 and refuses with a readable message when it is missing. On
    Windows 10 1903 and later that check always passes; on Windows 7 SP1 and 8.1 it frequently
    does not, and those are exactly the machines where telling a site-office user to "download
    .NET Framework 4.8 from Microsoft" is how a rollout stalls.

    A Burn bundle is the documented answer. It is a real executable with its own engine that
    installs prerequisites in order and then hands off to the MSI, and it is what every Microsoft
    redistributable you have ever run actually is. The output is one file:

        SEL.Agent-Setup-1.0.0.0.exe

    Everything needed is inside it. Nothing else has to be copied to the PC.

    ── One UAC prompt, not three ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Burn runs its user interface unelevated and asks for elevation once, at the point where it
    starts applying the chain. Every package after that — .NET, WebView2, the MSI — runs in that
    single elevated session.

    This is also what enforces "only an administrator can install this", and it does so far
    better than a property check. An administrator sees a consent prompt. A standard user sees a
    *credential* prompt, and can hand the keyboard to IT to type a password. Declining either one
    aborts the install. Windows enforces it, we do not have to, and the standard user is not left
    at a dead end — which is precisely what the `Privileged` launch condition removed from
    Package.wxs used to do.

    ── What is embedded, and what is not ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    .NET Framework 4.8 (121 MB) is embedded. It is mandatory: without it the agent does not start
    at all, so a rollout to an offline site office has to carry it.

    The Edge WebView2 runtime is embedded as its ~2 MB bootstrapper, which downloads the runtime
    when it runs. The full offline installer is 188 MB and would more than double the setup, for
    a component that is optional: WebView2 powers the ERP window *inside* the agent, and where it
    is absent ErpBrowser detects that and opens SEL LIVE in the user's normal browser instead.
    The feature is never silently dropped — the agent says which one it is using — so paying
    188 MB on every PC to avoid a fallback that works is the wrong trade.

    For genuinely air-gapped machines, build with -OfflineWebView2 and the full runtime is
    embedded instead. See build.ps1.
  -->

  <?ifndef Version ?>
  <?define Version = "1.0.0.0" ?>
  <?endif?>

  <!-- Set by build.ps1 to the WebView2 payload it staged: the bootstrapper, or the full
       offline runtime when -OfflineWebView2 was used. -->
  <?ifndef WebView2Payload ?>
  <?define WebView2Payload = "redist\MicrosoftEdgeWebview2Setup.exe" ?>
  <?endif?>

  <?ifndef WebView2Arguments ?>
  <?define WebView2Arguments = "/silent /install" ?>
  <?endif?>

  <?ifndef MsiPath ?>
  <?define MsiPath = "obj\SEL.Agent.msi" ?>
  <?endif?>

  <!--
    DisableModify and DisableRemove: no buttons beside this entry in Apps and Features.

    Removing the agent needs a SEL LIVE administrator's approval — the MSI asks for it, the same
    way the tray's Exit does. Leaving an Uninstall button there as well would be an invitation to
    the one route that used to skip that approval entirely, and the first thing anybody tries.

    Administrators remove it with the setup executable, which is the documented route in §5 and
    is what a deployment tool uses anyway:

        SEL.Agent-Setup-1.0.0.0.exe /uninstall            (asks for approval)
        SEL.Agent-Setup-1.0.0.0.exe /uninstall /quiet     (for a fleet rollback)

    This is deterrence, not enforcement: a local administrator can still stop the service and
    delete the folder. §7's position that the agent is not a security boundary is unchanged. What
    it buys is that the discoverable route produces a record naming who approved it.
  -->
  <Bundle Name="SEL LIVE Windows Agent"
          Manufacturer="Siddhartha Engineering Limited"
          Version="$(Version)"
          UpgradeCode="3A9F6C21-D847-4B05-9E7A-B6134F8C2D95"
          Compressed="yes"
          AboutUrl="https://seltech.store"
          IconSourceFile="..\SEL.Agent\app.ico"
          DisableModify="yes"
          DisableRemove="yes"
          Tag="SEL LIVE Agent">

    <!--
      A different UpgradeCode from the MSI's, deliberately.

      They are two distinct products to Windows: upgrading the bundle must not be mistaken for
      upgrading the package it carries. Sharing the code makes the bundle try to major-upgrade
      its own child and the chain fails in a way that is very hard to read in a log.
    -->

    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
        Theme="rtfLicense"
        ThemeFile="theme.xml"
        LocalizationFile="theme.wxl"
        LicenseFile="License.rtf"
        SuppressOptionsUI="yes"
        ShowVersion="yes" />
      <!--
        theme.xml is the stock rtfLicense theme with one checkbox added, taken from the built
        bundle rather than written from scratch so it stays byte-for-byte what WiX 5 ships apart
        from that line. A Checkbox binds to the Burn variable of its own name, which is how the
        desktop-shortcut choice reaches the MSI — the standard bootstrapper has no other way to
        put a question on screen.

        theme.wxl comes with it because the theme references localised strings by id; supplying
        one without the other leaves the buttons blank.
      -->
      <!--
        SuppressOptionsUI hides the "Options" button, which in the standard theme offers only an
        install folder picker. The agent's location is not a choice worth offering: the MSI's
        service registration, its ProgramData ACL and its auto-start entry all point at
        Program Files, and a user who moves it gains nothing and can break the update check.
      -->
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <!-- ══ Detection ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

         Read in the 32-bit registry view, because this bundle is x86. Both values are present
         in that view on 64-bit Windows: the .NET Framework NDP key is written to both views,
         and EdgeUpdate registers WebView2 under WOW6432Node. An x86 bundle therefore needs no
         bitness juggling to see a runtime installed by a 64-bit installer.
    -->

    <util:RegistrySearch Id="NetFx48Release"
                         Variable="NetFx48Release"
                         Root="HKLM"
                         Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\NET Framework Setup\NDP\v4\Full"
                         Value="Release"
                         Result="value" />

    <!-- WebView2 can be installed per-machine or per-user, and either satisfies the agent.
         Checking only HKLM would reinstall it for somebody who already has a working per-user
         copy, which on a locked-down PC is the copy they were allowed to have. -->
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="WebView2Machine"
                         Variable="WebView2Machine"
                         Root="HKLM"
                         Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\Clients\{F3017226-FE2A-4295-8BDF-00C3A9A7E4C5}"
                         Value="pv"
                         Result="value" />

    <util:RegistrySearch Id="WebView2User"
                         Variable="WebView2User"
                         Root="HKCU"
                         Key="Software\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\Clients\{F3017226-FE2A-4295-8BDF-00C3A9A7E4C5}"
                         Value="pv"
                         Result="value" />

    <!-- ══ Configuration, passed straight through to the MSI ══════════════════════════════════

         SEL.Agent-Setup.exe /quiet ENROLLMENTCODE=SEL-HO-2026

         APIBASEURL is defaulted by the MSI to the company's own server and the Firebase key is
         fetched from it, so an ordinary rollout passes neither. Pass APIBASEURL to point a
         pilot machine at a staging server instead.

         Identical spellings to the MSI properties, so one set of instructions covers both the
         bundle and a bare msiexec deployment. Overridable is what makes them settable on the
         command line at all; without it Burn ignores the assignment silently.
    -->
    <Variable Name="APIBASEURL"     Type="string" Value="" bal:Overridable="yes" />
    <Variable Name="FIREBASEAPIKEY" Type="string" Value="" bal:Overridable="yes" />
    <Variable Name="ENROLLMENTCODE" Type="string" Value="" bal:Overridable="yes" />
    <Variable Name="DEVICENAME"     Type="string" Value="" bal:Overridable="yes" />

    <!--
      The desktop shortcut, offered as a checkbox on the setup program's first page and passed
      through to the MSI. A numeric variable rather than a string because the theme's checkbox
      writes 0 or 1, and the MSI's component condition compares it as text either way.
    -->
    <Variable Name="DESKTOPSHORTCUT" Type="numeric" Value="1" bal:Overridable="yes" />

    <!-- ══ Supported releases ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

         Refused here rather than three packages later. The MSI carries the same conditions, but
         a bundle that installs 121 MB of .NET before discovering the OS is unsupported has
         wasted ten minutes of somebody's morning.

         Burn version literals are vVersionNT-style: v6.1 is Windows 7, v6.2 Windows 8.0,
         v6.3 Windows 8.1, v10.0 Windows 10 and 11.
    -->
    <bal:Condition
      Condition="VersionNT &gt;= v6.1"
      Message="The SEL LIVE Agent supports Windows 7 SP1, Windows 8.1, Windows 10 and Windows 11. This PC is running an older release of Windows." />

    <bal:Condition
      Condition="NOT (VersionNT = v6.1 AND ServicePackLevel &lt; 1)"
      Message="Windows 7 needs Service Pack 1 before the SEL LIVE Agent can be installed. Install SP1 from Windows Update and run this setup again." />

    <bal:Condition
      Condition="VersionNT &lt;&gt; v6.2"
      Message="Windows 8.0 cannot run .NET Framework 4.8, which the SEL LIVE Agent requires. Update this PC to Windows 8.1 — it is a free update from the Store — and run this setup again." />

    <!-- ══ The chain ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <Chain>

      <!--
        .NET Framework 4.8.

        Permanent, because it is a shared Windows component. Uninstalling the agent must not
        remove the framework three other applications on the PC have started depending on since.

        Protocol="netfx4" is not cosmetic: it is how Burn reads the framework installer's own
        progress pipe, so the bar moves during the several minutes this takes on Windows 7
        rather than sitting at zero and looking hung.

        Vital, because there is no agent without it.
      -->
      <ExePackage Id="NetFx48"
                  DisplayName="Microsoft .NET Framework 4.8"
                  Description="Required by the SEL LIVE Agent"
                  Permanent="yes"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Protocol="netfx4"
                  DetectCondition="NetFx48Release &gt;= 528040"
                  InstallArguments="/q /norestart /ChainingPackage &quot;SEL LIVE Agent&quot;">
        <ExePackagePayload SourceFile="redist\NDP48-x86-x64-AllOS-ENU.exe" />

        <!--
          The framework installer asks for a restart far more often than it needs one, and on
          Windows 7 it nearly always does. scheduleReboot lets the rest of the chain finish and
          defers the prompt to the end, so the agent is installed and configured before the PC
          goes down rather than after a reboot nobody performs until Friday.
        -->
        <ExitCode Value="3010" Behavior="scheduleReboot" />
        <ExitCode Value="1641" Behavior="scheduleReboot" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!--
        The Edge WebView2 runtime, for the ERP window inside the agent.

        Vital="no" is the important attribute. This is the one package in the chain whose failure
        must not fail the install: WebView2 has no Microsoft support on Windows 7 or 8.1, needs
        network access when installed from the bootstrapper, and is blocked outright by some
        managed-desktop policies. In every one of those cases ErpBrowser probes for it at
        startup, does not find it, says so in the agent log and in the status panel, and opens
        SEL LIVE in the user's default browser. The capability survives; only its delivery
        changes. Refusing to install an attendance agent because an optional browser control is
        unavailable would be the wrong failure.

        InstallCondition skips it on Windows 7 and 8.1 rather than running an installer Microsoft
        stopped supporting there in 2023, whose failure mode on those releases is less
        predictable than not running it.
      -->
      <ExePackage Id="WebView2Runtime"
                  DisplayName="Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime"
                  Description="Lets SEL LIVE open inside the agent window (optional)"
                  Permanent="yes"
                  Vital="no"
                  DetectCondition="WebView2Machine OR WebView2User"
                  InstallCondition="VersionNT &gt;= v10.0"
                  InstallArguments="$(WebView2Arguments)">
        <ExePackagePayload SourceFile="$(WebView2Payload)" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!--
        The agent.

        Visible="no" (the default for MsiPackage) keeps the MSI out of Programs and Features, so
        there is one uninstall entry — the bundle's — rather than two, one of which half-removes
        the product and leaves the other behind.

        The MSI's own WixUI_Minimal never appears. Burn always runs a chained MSI at the basic
        UI level unless the package opts in, and it should not: the bundle has already shown a
        licence and a progress bar, and two sets of UI for one install is how a setup program
        comes to look amateur. (WiX 3 spelled this DisplayInternalUI="no" on this element;
        WiX 4 removed the attribute and made silent the only behaviour.)
      -->
      <MsiPackage Id="SelAgentMsi"
                  DisplayName="SEL LIVE Windows Agent"
                  SourceFile="$(MsiPath)"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Compressed="yes">
        <MsiProperty Name="APIBASEURL"     Value="[APIBASEURL]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="FIREBASEAPIKEY" Value="[FIREBASEAPIKEY]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="ENROLLMENTCODE" Value="[ENROLLMENTCODE]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="DEVICENAME"     Value="[DEVICENAME]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="DESKTOPSHORTCUT" Value="[DESKTOPSHORTCUT]" />
      </MsiPackage>

    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
